## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to implement per-tenant routing through ConfigMaps, path rewriting, and versioning with safe defaults.

## Key Concepts
- Namespace-scoped ConfigMaps for feature flags
- Request rewriting via tenant header
- Versioning strategy and default fallback
- Minimal YAML manifests for end-to-end validation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo rewrite function
function rewrite(req, tenantId, version, mapping) {
  const base = mapping[tenantId] || mapping['default'];
  req.url = `/v${version || '1'}/api${req.path}`;
  req.headers['Accept'] = 'application/json';
  req.headers['Host'] = base.host;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test unknown tenants and version upgrades?
- How to handle partial mappings and rollback?

## Why This Is Asked
Real-world traffic shaping at tenant level requires deterministic routing and easy rollback.

## Key Concepts
- Hash-based per-tenant routing
- Namespace ConfigMaps for feature flags
- Canary percentage control and target routing
- Production fallback and validation endpoints

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo: determine if a request should go to canary
const enabled = getConfig('cnf.canary.enabled');
const pct = getConfig('cnf.canary.percentage');
if (enabled) {
  const h = hash(`${tenantId}${path}`) % 100;
  routeToCanary = h < pct;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you implement safe rollback if canary metrics drop?
- How to simulate traffic split in tests and monitor drift?

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests practical integration of workload identity with policy enforcement in a CNF gateway, emphasizing data governance, per-tenant isolation, and non-disruptive upgrades. It requires concrete steps for credential rotation, policy encoding, runtime checks, and observability.

## Key Concepts

- SPIRE SVIDs and workload attestation for multitenant isolation
- OPA Gatekeeper policy modeling per tenant (retention windows, redaction rules)
- Runtime log redaction and per-tenant observability
- Kind-based local testbed for end-to-end verification
- Zero-downtime rollout with traffic shaping and drift checks

## Code Example

```javascript
function redactLog(entry, policy) {
  const redacted = { ...entry };
  (policy.redactFields || []).forEach(field => {
    if (redacted.hasOwnProperty(field)) redacted[field] = "***REDACTED***";
  });
  return redacted;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you rotate SVIDs across tenants with revocation semantics in SPIRE?
- How would you validate redaction coverage across logs in CI/CD and production?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical end-to-end config provenance and policy enforcement for multi-tenant CNF platforms, a common production risk area beyond image provenance.

## Key Concepts
- Config payload signing with Cosign and KMS-backed keys
- SBOM attachment for config changes
- Admission control with Kyverno/OPA enforcing signature, SBOM, and tenant labels
- Canary namespace validation before rollout
- Per-tenant rollback mechanisms and status surfacing

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo workflow: verify config update integrity before applying
async function verifyConfigUpdate(payload, signature, sbom) {
  const okSig = await verifySignature(payload, signature);
  const okSBOM = await validateSBOM(payload, sbom);
  const tenantOk = checkTenantLabel(payload);
  return okSig && okSBOM && tenantOk;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test rollback semantics across tenants?
- How would you handle SBOM drift when multiple tenants update concurrently?
